# Cloudflare Pages rewrites. Vite copies public/ verbatim into dist/, so this ships with the build.
#
# Every rule here is a 200 "proxy": the browser keeps the URL it asked for (query string included)
# and Pages answers with the body of /spa-shell, the blank document scripts/spaShell.mjs describes.
#
# THESE RULES ARE WHAT KEEPS THE APP REACHABLE. The build has a top-level 404.html (the not-found
# page, scripts/prerender.mjs), so a path with no file and no rule here is answered with that page
# and HTTP 404. Without the rules below that is /auth, an emailed /reset-password link and every
# page under /app. (They were first added for a smaller reason: before 404.html existed such a path
# got index.html, the prerendered HOMEPAGE, which then sat on screen until the bundle ran.)
#
# Rules for editing this file. Each was read from Cloudflare's parser and asset handler (wrangler
# 4.146.0: workers-shared parseRedirects, pages-shared asset-server/handler) and measured on a Pages
# preview (the trailing-slash one in the local Pages emulator only); scripts/spaShell.test.ts
# enforces them.
#   - List only routes that exist in the browser alone. A rule is applied BEFORE the file lookup, so
#     one that matched "/" or a prerendered page (/privacy, /terms, ...) would replace real, crawlable
#     HTML with a blank noindex document.
#   - The destination is /spa-shell, never /spa-shell.html. Pages answers an .html destination with a
#     308 to the extensionless URL instead of serving it. (That, and the parser dropping
#     "/* /index.html 200" as an infinite loop, is why 200 rewrites were once recorded here as "not
#     honoured" -- see functions/assets/[[path]].ts.)
#   - Exact paths go above the splat rule. Once the parser has seen a splat it files every later rule
#     as dynamic, which is slower and counts against a limit of 100 rather than 2,000.
#   - An exact rule does not cover its trailing-slash form, hence the pairs. React Router treats
#     /auth/ as /auth, so both are real addresses.
#   - A new browser-only route needs a line here, in both forms (the /app/* splat already covers
#     anything under /app). Forgetting IS an outage for that route: a direct visit, a reload or an
#     emailed link to it returns 404 and paints the not-found page, and only a visitor whose browser
#     runs the bundle then sees the real page replace it. The test fails so that it cannot ship.
#   - Matching is case-sensitive. /Auth has no rule and gets the 404 page; React Router, which is
#     not case-sensitive, then mounts the sign-in page over it. Not worth a rule per spelling.
#   - A rule with no route behind it is a soft 404 (the shell, 200, then React's not-found page), so
#     a line goes when its route goes. The /app/* splat does this for an unknown address under /app.
#     That one is accepted: robots.txt disallows /app and the shell is noindex.
#
# Unknown paths are deliberately not rewritten: they get 404.html and a real 404, which is the point
# of that file. There is also no rule that could mean "everything else" without matching the pages
# that have files.

/auth /spa-shell 200
/auth/ /spa-shell 200
/login /spa-shell 200
/login/ /spa-shell 200
/register /spa-shell 200
/register/ /spa-shell 200
/forgot-password /spa-shell 200
/forgot-password/ /spa-shell 200
/reset-password /spa-shell 200
/reset-password/ /spa-shell 200
/verify-email /spa-shell 200
/verify-email/ /spa-shell 200
/verify-phone /spa-shell 200
/verify-phone/ /spa-shell 200
/email-change-verify /spa-shell 200
/email-change-verify/ /spa-shell 200
/app /spa-shell 200
/app/* /spa-shell 200
